        "---\n",
        "title: \"Positional Encoding and Sequence Geometry\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"How order enters attention-based language models\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Self-attention compares tokens by content.  If the input to a Transformer is only a matrix of token embeddings, then self-attention sees a set of token vectors rather than an ordered sentence.  This is a serious mathematical problem: the sentences\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{the dog chased the cat}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{the cat chased the dog}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "contain nearly the same words, but they have different meanings because their order is different.  Positional encoding is the mechanism by which a Transformer receives information about order.\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter studies position from a mathematical point of view.  We begin with permutation equivariance, then study absolute positional embeddings, sinusoidal positional encodings, Fourier-feature geometry, relative positions, rotary positional embeddings, and attention biases.  The goal is not to memorize architectural variations, but to understand what mathematical information about order is injected into a sequence model.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. explain why vanilla self-attention without position is permutation equivariant;\n",
        "2. distinguish token embeddings, position embeddings, and contextual embeddings;\n",
        "3. write the sinusoidal positional encoding formulas and check their dimensions;\n",
        "4. explain sinusoidal encoding as a finite collection of Fourier features;\n",
        "5. compute dot products between sinusoidal position vectors and interpret them as functions of relative distance;\n",
        "6. compare learned absolute position embeddings with fixed sinusoidal encodings;\n",
        "7. describe relative-position attention, rotary positional embeddings, and additive attention biases;\n",
        "8. implement positional encodings in NumPy;\n",
        "9. visualize position vectors and their similarity structure with Plotly.js;\n",
        "10. identify common mathematical mistakes involving position in Transformers.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.1 The order problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Let a sequence of length $T$ be represented by a matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X =\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "- & x_1^T & - \\\\\n",
        "- & x_2^T & - \\\\\n",
        "  & \\vdots &   \\\\\n",
        "- & x_T^T & -\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in R^{T\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $x_t\\in R^d$ is the input representation of the token at position $t$.  A self-attention layer computes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Attn}(X)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\n",
        "\\frac{XW_Q(XW_K)^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "\\right)XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula compares rows of $X$ using dot products.  If the rows are permuted, the dot-product matrix is permuted in the same way.  Thus self-attention respects the symmetry of the input row order unless we explicitly break that symmetry by adding position information.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Key issue.**  A sequence is not only a multiset of tokens.  A sequence has an order.  A Transformer must therefore represent both content and position.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, the two sequences\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(w_1,w_2,w_3)=(\\text{students},\\text{use},\\text{AI})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(w_1,w_2,w_3)=(\\text{AI},\\text{use},\\text{students})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "have the same bag of words but different syntax and meaning.  Bag-of-words models from Chapters 2--3 intentionally discard order.  Transformers should not.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.2 Permutation equivariance of self-attention\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $P\\in R^{T\\times T}$ be a permutation matrix.  Multiplying $X$ on the left by $P$ permutes the rows of $X$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X' = PX.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a single self-attention head without position, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q=XW_Q,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K=XW_K,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V=XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the permuted input $X'=PX$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q'=PXW_Q=PQ,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K'=PK,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V'=PV.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The score matrix becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S'\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{Q'(K')^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{(PQ)(PK)^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "P\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)P^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The row-wise softmax satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(PSP^T)\n",
        "=\n",
        "P\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S)P^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\operatorname{Attn}(PX)\n",
        "&=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S')V' \\\\\n",
        "&=\n",
        "P\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S)P^T PV \\\\\n",
        "&=\n",
        "P\\operatorname{Attn}(X).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So self-attention is **permutation equivariant**:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Attn}(PX)=P\\operatorname{Attn}(X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not wrong; it is often a useful symmetry.  But for language, the original row index matters.  Positional encoding breaks pure permutation symmetry by replacing the token-only representation with a content-plus-position representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.3 Content vectors plus position vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $e:V\\to R^d$ be a token embedding map.  If token $w_t$ appears at position $t$, the token embedding is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_t=e(w_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple positional strategy creates a vector $p_t\\in R^d$ for every position $t$, then uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t = x_t + p_t.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The matrix input to the Transformer is then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z = X + P_{\\text{pos}},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_{\\text{pos}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "- & p_1^T & - \\\\\n",
        "- & p_2^T & - \\\\\n",
        "  & \\vdots &   \\\\\n",
        "- & p_T^T & -\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in R^{T\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The addition is simple but mathematically powerful.  Because the vector at row $t$ now contains both token identity and position identity, attention scores can depend on position through inner products such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(z_i W_Q)(z_j W_K)^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Expanding $z_i=x_i+p_i$ and $z_j=x_j+p_j$ shows the possible interactions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(z_i W_Q)(z_j W_K)^T\n",
        "=\n",
        "(x_iW_Q)(x_jW_K)^T\n",
        "+\n",
        "(x_iW_Q)(p_jW_K)^T\n",
        "+\n",
        "(p_iW_Q)(x_jW_K)^T\n",
        "+\n",
        "(p_iW_Q)(p_jW_K)^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These terms allow the model to learn rules involving content, source position, target position, and relative placement.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.4 Learned absolute position embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "The most direct approach is to learn a position-embedding matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E_{\\text{pos}}\\in R^{T_{\\max}\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where row $t$ is the learned vector $p_t^T$.  For a sequence of length $T\\le T_{\\max}$, use the first $T$ rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "The trainable parameters are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "T_{\\max}d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This method is flexible because the model learns the position vectors that help the training objective.  But it has two limitations.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, the model is usually trained with a fixed maximum length $T_{\\max}$.  Extrapolating beyond this length is not natural unless additional design choices are made.\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, learned absolute embeddings do not automatically encode a smooth geometry of distance.  The model must learn from data that positions 10 and 11 are closer than positions 10 and 500.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Absolute position viewpoint.**  Learned absolute position embeddings identify each index $t$ with a trainable vector $p_t$.  They are flexible labels for positions, but they do not automatically impose translation or distance structure.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.5 Sinusoidal positional encoding\n",
        "\n",
        "The original Transformer introduced a fixed sinusoidal positional encoding.  Suppose $d$ is even.  For position $t$ and coordinate pair indexed by $i=0,1,\\ldots,d/2-1$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "PE(t,2i)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{t}{10000^{2i/d}}\\right),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "PE(t,2i+1)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{t}{10000^{2i/d}}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus each position $t$ is mapped to a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t = PE(t)\\in R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The denominator\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "10000^{2i/d}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "creates wavelengths on a logarithmic scale.  Low-index coordinate pairs vary rapidly with $t$; high-index coordinate pairs vary slowly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently, define frequencies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\omega_i = 10000^{-2i/d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the coordinate pair is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(\\sin(\\omega_i t),\\cos(\\omega_i t)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This shows the Fourier-feature structure: sinusoidal encoding represents each position through multiple periodic functions at different frequencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.6 Geometry of sinusoidal encodings\n",
        "\n",
        "For a single frequency $\\omega$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_t(\\omega)=(\\sin(\\omega t),\\cos(\\omega t))\\in R^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|u_t(\\omega)\\|_2^2\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sin^2(\\omega t)+\\cos^2(\\omega t)=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The dot product between two positions is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "u_t(\\omega)^T u_s(\\omega)\n",
        "&=\\sin(\\omega t)\\sin(\\omega s)+\\cos(\\omega t)\\cos(\\omega s)\\\\\n",
        "&=\\cos(\\omega(t-s)).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore the similarity between two sinusoidal position vectors depends on the relative displacement $t-s$.  With multiple frequencies,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t^T p_s\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_i \\cos(\\omega_i(t-s)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a crucial mathematical property: although the encoding is absolute because it assigns a vector to each $t$, inner products between position vectors naturally contain relative-distance information.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Relative-distance property.**  For sinusoidal encodings, the dot product $p_t^T p_s$ is a function of $t-s$.  Thus the geometry of position vectors contains relative offset information.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.7 Linear transformations of sinusoidal encodings\n",
        "\n",
        "There is another useful property.  For a fixed shift $\\Delta$, the pair\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(\\sin(\\omega(t+\\Delta)),\\cos(\\omega(t+\\Delta)))\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "can be obtained from\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(\\sin(\\omega t),\\cos(\\omega t))\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "by a two-dimensional rotation.  Using angle-addition formulas,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sin(\\omega(t+\\Delta))\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sin(\\omega t)\\cos(\\omega\\Delta)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega t)\\sin(\\omega\\Delta),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega(t+\\Delta))\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega t)\\cos(\\omega\\Delta)\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\sin(\\omega t)\\sin(\\omega\\Delta).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In matrix form,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\sin(\\omega(t+\\Delta))\\\\\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega(t+\\Delta))\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega\\Delta) & \\sin(\\omega\\Delta)\\\\\n",
        "-\\sin(\\omega\\Delta) & \\cos(\\omega\\Delta)\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\sin(\\omega t)\\\\\n",
        "\\cos(\\omega t)\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So a fixed relative shift is represented by a block-diagonal linear map across the sinusoidal coordinate pairs.  This helps explain why sinusoidal encodings can support relative-position reasoning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.8 Scaling token embeddings and position embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "In many Transformer implementations, token embeddings are scaled before adding positions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t=\\sqrt{d}\\,x_t+p_t.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The reason is variance control.  Suppose the coordinates of $x_t$ have typical scale around $1/\\sqrt{d}$.  Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|x_t\\|_2\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "may be order 1.  Multiplying by $\\sqrt{d}$ changes the coordinate scale and can balance the token content against the added positional signal, depending on how embeddings are initialized.\n",
        "\n",
        "From a mathematical perspective, the relative magnitude of $x_t$ and $p_t$ matters because attention scores involve dot products.  If $p_t$ is too small, the model may ignore order.  If $p_t$ is too large, the model may overemphasize position and underemphasize content.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.9 Relative position representations\n",
        "\n",
        "Absolute position embeddings assign vectors to positions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "t\\mapsto p_t.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Relative-position methods instead emphasize offsets:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(i,j)\\mapsto r_{i-j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In attention, a relative-position term may be added to the score between query position $i$ and key position $j$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S_{ij}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{q_i^T k_j}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "+\n",
        " b_{i-j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $b_{i-j}$ is a learned or prescribed scalar bias depending on relative displacement.  This gives the model direct access to information such as \"nearby tokens should often matter more\" or \"previous tokens matter in a causal language model.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "A vector-valued version uses relative embeddings $r_{i-j}\\in R^{d_k}$, for example\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S_{ij}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{q_i^T(k_j+r_{i-j})}{\\sqrt{d_k}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The main idea is that attention scores become functions of both token similarity and relative location.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.10 Rotary positional embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "Rotary positional embeddings, often abbreviated RoPE, encode position by rotating query and key vectors.  The simplest version applies a two-dimensional rotation to each coordinate pair.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a vector pair $(a,b)$ and angle $\\theta$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R_\\theta\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}a\\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\cos\\theta & -\\sin\\theta\\\\\n",
        "\\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}a\\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "At position $t$, coordinate pair $i$ is rotated by angle\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{t,i}=\\omega_i t.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $R_t$ denote the resulting block-diagonal rotation matrix.  RoPE uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tilde q_t = R_t q_t,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\tilde k_s = R_s k_s.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The attention score uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tilde q_t^T\\tilde k_s\n",
        "=\n",
        "q_t^T R_t^T R_s k_s.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Because rotations satisfy\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R_t^T R_s = R_{s-t},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the score depends on the relative offset $s-t$ through the rotation matrix.  Thus RoPE builds relative position directly into dot-product attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**RoPE viewpoint.**  Instead of adding a position vector to the token vector, RoPE rotates query and key coordinates.  Dot products between rotated vectors naturally encode relative displacement.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.11 Attention bias and distance decay\n",
        "\n",
        "Another common approach is to add a deterministic or learned distance bias to attention scores:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S_{ij}\n",
        "=\\frac{q_i^T k_j}{\\sqrt{d_k}} + b(i,j).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a causal decoder, $b(i,j)$ may include the mask\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b(i,j)=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "0, & j\\le i,\\\\\n",
        "-\\infty, & j>i.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A distance-decay bias may take the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b(i,j)=-\\lambda |i-j|,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "or, in causal form,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b(i,j)=-\\lambda(i-j)\\quad \\text{for } j\\le i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This bias makes attention to distant positions less likely unless the content score is strong enough to overcome the penalty.  From the perspective of probability, the row-wise softmax turns scores into a distribution over positions, so additive biases act like log-prior preferences over where attention should be placed.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.12 Positional encoding as feature design\n",
        "\n",
        "Before deep learning, feature engineering was a central part of NLP.  Positional encoding is a modern version of feature design.  It chooses a map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\psi:\\{1,2,\\ldots,T_{\\max}\\}\\to R^d\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and combines it with the token embedding map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e:V\\to R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The model receives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t=e(w_t)+\\psi(t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Different choices of $\\psi$ encode different assumptions:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | Mathematical object | Strength | Limitation |\n",
        "|---|---:|---|---|\n",
        "| Learned absolute | trainable table $E_{\\text{pos}}$ | flexible | weak extrapolation |\n",
        "| Sinusoidal | Fourier features | relative-offset geometry | fixed frequencies |\n",
        "| Relative bias | scalar $b_{i-j}$ | direct distance modeling | less content-position interaction |\n",
        "| RoPE | block rotations $R_t$ | relative offset in dot products | requires paired dimensions |\n",
        "| Linear bias | monotone score penalty | simple length behavior | less expressive |\n",
        "\n",
        "The correct choice depends on the task, sequence length, training data, and architecture.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.13 Interactive: sinusoidal position geometry\n",
        "\n",
