        "\n",
        "## 16.21 Common mistakes\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Confusing token order with vocabulary order.**  The vocabulary index of a token is not its sentence position.\n",
        "2. **Forgetting that self-attention is permutation equivariant without position.**  Attention alone does not create order information.\n",
        "3. **Using mismatched dimensions.**  If token and position vectors are added, both must lie in $R^d$.\n",
        "4. **Assuming sinusoidal encoding is random.**  It is deterministic and structured.\n",
        "5. **Assuming all positional methods are equivalent.**  Absolute embeddings, relative biases, and RoPE affect the model in different mathematical ways.\n",
        "6. **Ignoring sequence length.**  A method that works for short sequences may behave differently for long-context modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 16.22 Worked calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $d=4$ and frequencies $\\omega_0=1$, $\\omega_1=0.1$.  The sinusoidal position vector is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t=(\\sin t,\\cos t,\\sin(0.1t),\\cos(0.1t)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t^Tp_s\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\cos(t-s)+\\cos(0.1(t-s)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In particular,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_3^Tp_1\n",
        "=\\cos(2)+\\cos(0.2).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The result depends on $3-1=2$, not on the absolute pair $(3,1)$ separately.  This illustrates how sinusoidal encodings provide relative-offset information through inner products.\n",
        "\n",
